WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.560
A Clockwork Orange, based on Anthony Burgess's novel of the same name, is one

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.180
of the best — and most controversial — movies
of the 1970s.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.720
Despite the arguments surrounding the film's
violence, this Stanley Kubrick

00:00:11.731 --> 00:00:15.599
picture has become an iconic piece of pop
culture, and multiple directors and showrunners

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.860
have since paid tribute to it in their own
works.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:22.080
So pour yourself a glass of milk as we delve
into some of our favorite Clockwork Orange

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:22.880
Easter eggs.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:23.980
Hellraiser

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.540
Directed by horror icon Clive Barker, Hellraiser
is a horror classic that gave the world Pinhead,

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:31.860
one of the all-time great movie monsters.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.840
The film also contains a Clockwork Orange
reference that's so quick that it's easy to

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:36.340
miss.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.160
In the pic the Cotton House is located at
55 Ludovico Place, which

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.000
is a reference to the infamous torture technique
that Alex experiences in Kubrick's masterpiece.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.200
"Well that's good.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.440
I like to video the old films now and again."

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:49.820
Trainspotting

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.540
Like A Clockwork Orange, Trainspotting is
also a masterclass of British cinema, and

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.000
it includes quite a few nods to Kubrick's
tour de force.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.380
Not only did Danny Boyle design the film's
Volcano nightclub to look reminiscent of Clockwork's

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.860
Korova Milk Bar with all the
writing on the wall, but the background music

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.180
was played by Heaven 17, a band that was named
after a fictional pop group from the Anthony

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.060
Burgess novel.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.260
Star Trek: The Next Generation

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.430
In the fourth season of Star Trek: The Next
Generation, an episode called "The Mind's

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:20.060
Eye" featured the Romulans
using their own version of the Ludovico technique.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.880
Since Geordi La Forge is blind,
they have to pump images of violence straight

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.700
into his brain, which is somehow even creepier
than what happens to Alex in Clockwork.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.160
"I can tell you've gone to a lot of trouble."

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:32.960
"Indeed we have."

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:34.520
The Dark Knight

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.440
While Heath Ledger was playing the Joker in
The Dark Knight, he kept a creepy journal

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.420
of all of his influences and thoughts to prepare
for production.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.800
It's probably not too surprising that Alex
DeLarge was a source of inspiration for Heath

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.310
Ledger's performance as The Joker — because
they're both sadistic psychopaths who want

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:50.280
to see the world burn.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.180
"Well hello beautiful"

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.640
But what might come as a shock is how he managed
to sneak in a subtle nod to Clockwork and

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.160
its director with his own version of the Kubrick
stare.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.240
The Kubrick stare is a nod to the fact that,
at some point in nearly all of his movies,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.620
the director has one of the characters stare
at the camera with their head tilted down,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.740
looking up from under the eyebrows, with a
crazed look in their eyes.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.800
It shows up quite often in Clockwork, so it's
probably no coincidence that after being so

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:21.560
influenced by Alex DeLarge, Ledger borrowed
that iconic piece of Malcolm Mcdowell's performance

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.060
as well.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:23.600
Teen Titans

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:28.940
A recurring villain in the DC universe, Mad
Mod is an eccentric fashion designer who uses

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.620
his style and technology to brainwash and
wreak havoc.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.980
And in the animated series from 2003 and the
revival in 2017, the character was voiced

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.380
by none other than Malcolm McDowell himself.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:41.360
"Will you ever learn?

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.320
You're in my world, my duckies.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.460
You can't win"

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:50.020
To celebrate his involvement, the show goes
full-bore on A Clockwork Orange Easter eggs

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.400
during his guest stints.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.220
Mad Mod forces the Teen Titans to watch mind
control videos, and then he also appears alongside

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.320
a ton of weird psychedelic imagery that wouldn't
look out of place in the movie that made him

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:00.820
famous.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.100
"Can't have those nasty superpowers disrupting
my legend now can I?"

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.080
American Gods

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.180
The first episode of American Gods gave us
all the great characters and bloody mayhem

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.880
that we've come to expect from TV producer
Bryan Fuller.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:18.220
One thing fans really liked was the design
of the Technical Boy and his minions, the

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:18.740
Children.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.160
Perhaps the reason they were so popular was
that they were familiar to Clockwork fans

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:23.340
as a nod to the droogs.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:28.340
Just like the droogs, the Children wear two-toned
jumpsuits and enjoy being silent and violent.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.840
"That's part of the new way."

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:32.840
Thanks for watching!

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.239
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:37.400
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

